<article class="article-container">
    <h1 class="serif-heading mb-3" style="font-size: 2.8rem; color: var(--text-main);">The Nine Major Hadith Collections</h1>
    <div class="article-meta">
        <span><i class="fas fa-folder me-1"></i> Core Sacred Sciences</span>
        <span><i class="fas fa-clock me-1"></i> 20 Min Read</span>
        <span><i class="fas fa-user me-1"></i> By Noor Al-Huda Team</span>
    </div>

    <div class="article-content">
        <p class="lead">
            In the centuries after the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, scholars embarked on a monumental effort to preserve his words and actions. This culminated in the compilation of <strong>major Hadith collections</strong>, which serve as the primary sources of the Sunnah. Among these, nine specific collections—the <em>Six Canonical Books</em> and three foundational supplementary works—stand as the pillars of authentic Islamic tradition.
        </p>

        <div class="info-box gold">
            <h4><i class="fas fa-book-open"></i> What is a "Major Collection"?</h4>
            <p class="mb-0">
                A major Hadith collection is a book compiled by a respected Imam who dedicated their life to collecting, scrutinizing, and organizing the sayings of the Prophet ﷺ. The "Nine Books" collectively contain tens of thousands of narrations, filtered through rigorous conditions of narrators' integrity (<em>Adalah</em>) and precision (<em>Dabt</em>).
            </p>
        </div>

        <h3>The Six Canonical Books (Al-Kutub al-Sittah)</h3>
        <p>
            The six primary works are considered the most authentic and authoritative books after the Quran. They form the bedrock of Islamic jurisprudence and theology. While <em>Sahih al-Bukhari</em> and <em>Sahih Muslim</em> are the absolute gold standard, the other four are indispensable for legal rulings.
        </p>

        <div class="list-styled">
            <div class="list-item-styled" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--emerald-green);">
                <div class="list-icon"><i class="fas fa-star text-warning"></i></div>
                <div>
                    <h5 class="mb-2 serif-heading">1. Sahih Al-Bukhari</h5>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-1"><strong>Author:</strong> Imam Muhammad ibn Ismail al-Bukhari (d. 256 AH)</p>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-0">The most authentic book after the Quran. Imam Bukhari sifted through over 600,000 narrations to include only ~7,275 (with repetitions). He set the highest standard of verification, requiring that a narrator's teacher and the teacher's teacher must have definitively met and heard the Hadith.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="list-item-styled" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--deep-sky-blue);">
                <div class="list-icon"><i class="fas fa-star text-warning"></i></div>
                <div>
                    <h5 class="mb-2 serif-heading">2. Sahih Muslim</h5>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-1"><strong>Author:</strong> Imam Muslim ibn al-Hajjaj (d. 261 AH)</p>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-0">Often paired with Bukhari, this collection is praised for its incredible organization. Imam Muslim focused on grouping narrations by the exact wording rather than chapter topics, making it easier to compare variations. It contains ~12,000 narrations (with repetitions) and is considered the second most authentic book.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="list-item-styled" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--magenta);">
                <div class="list-icon"><i class="fas fa-gavel"></i></div>
                <div>
                    <h5 class="mb-2 serif-heading">3. Sunan Abu Dawud</h5>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-1"><strong>Author:</strong> Imam Abu Dawud al-Sijistani (d. 275 AH)</p>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-0">This collection was specifically designed for jurists (<em>Fuqaha</em>). Imam Abu Dawud focused on Hadiths that deal with legal rulings (<em>Ahkam</em>). He gathered ~4,800 narrations and carefully distinguished between authentic and weaker ones, making it a foundational reference for Islamic law.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="list-item-styled" style="border-left: 4px solid #ffc107;">
                <div class="list-icon"><i class="fas fa-feather-alt"></i></div>
                <div>
                    <h5 class="mb-2 serif-heading">4. Jami' At-Tirmidhi</h5>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-1"><strong>Author:</strong> Imam Muhammad ibn Isa al-Tirmidhi (d. 279 AH)</p>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-0">Also known as <em>Sunan al-Tirmidhi</em>, this is a unique collection because Imam al-Tirmidhi personally graded each Hadith as <em>Sahih</em>, <em>Hasan</em> (Good), or <em>Da'if</em> (Weak). His commentary on the narrators and the subtle differences in chains makes this book an absolute treasure for students of Hadith sciences.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="list-item-styled" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--deep-sky-blue);">
                <div class="list-icon"><i class="fas fa-check-double"></i></div>
                <div>
                    <h5 class="mb-2 serif-heading">5. Sunan An-Nasa'i</h5>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-1"><strong>Author:</strong> Imam Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb al-Nasa'i (d. 303 AH)</p>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-0">Known for having the most rigorous standards in verifying the chain of narrators after Bukhari and Muslim. Imam al-Nasa'i traveled extensively and critically analyzed narrators, often exposing hidden flaws in chains that others missed. His compilation is highly respected for its precision.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="list-item-styled" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--emerald-green);">
                <div class="list-icon"><i class="fas fa-link"></i></div>
                <div>
                    <h5 class="mb-2 serif-heading">6. Sunan Ibn Majah</h5>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-1"><strong>Author:</strong> Imam Muhammad ibn Majah al-Qazwini (d. 273 AH)</p>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-0">The sixth of the canonical books. While some scholars criticized it for containing a few weak narrations not found elsewhere, it remains widely accepted. It is invaluable because it contains many supplementary Hadiths regarding legal rulings that were omitted by the other five compilers.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3>The Complementary Major Works</h3>
        <p>
            In addition to the six canonical books, three other collections are considered essential for any advanced student of knowledge. These works offer unique perspectives and larger samples of the Prophetic legacy.
        </p>

        <div class="list-styled">
            <div class="list-item-styled" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--magenta);">
                <div class="list-icon"><i class="fas fa-tree"></i></div>
                <div>
                    <h5 class="mb-2 serif-heading">7. Musnad Ahmad ibn Hanbal</h5>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-1"><strong>Author:</strong> Imam Ahmad ibn Hanbal (d. 241 AH)</p>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-0">This is an enormous collection containing over 30,000 narrations! Rather than grouping by topic, Imam Ahmad organized it by <em>Companion</em> (Musnad). This makes it a vital source for tracking which Companions narrated what, and it contains many authentic Hadiths not found in the Six Books.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="list-item-styled" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--emerald-green);">
                <div class="list-icon"><i class="fas fa-mosque"></i></div>
                <div>
                    <h5 class="mb-2 serif-heading">8. Al-Muwatta of Imam Malik</h5>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-1"><strong>Author:</strong> Imam Malik ibn Anas (d. 179 AH)</p>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-0">This is the earliest surviving collection of Hadith and Fiqh. Imam Malik filtered over 10,000 narrations down to ~1,700. He heavily relied on the practice of the people of Medina (<em>Amal Ahl al-Madinah</em>), considering the city's communal practice as a living Sunnah inherited from the Companions.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="list-item-styled" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--deep-sky-blue);">
                <div class="list-icon"><i class="fas fa-scroll"></i></div>
                <div>
                    <h5 class="mb-2 serif-heading">9. Sunan Al-Darimi</h5>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-1"><strong>Author:</strong> Imam Abdullah ibn Abdur-Rahman al-Darimi (d. 255 AH)</p>
                    <p class="small text-muted mb-0">One of the earliest compilations, predating the Six Books. Imam al-Darimi traveled extensively and collected ~3,500 narrations. His book is particularly valuable because it preserves many early chains and is a critical resource for understanding the evolution of Hadith criticism.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3>The Science of Grading (Mustalah)</h3>
        <p>
            These nine collections are not considered "perfect" in the sense that every single narration is authentic. Scholars of <em>Mustalah al-Hadith</em> (Authentication) have studied every chain within these books for over a thousand years. 
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Bukhari & Muslim:</strong> Almost entirely <em>Sahih</em> (Authentic), with only a handful of weak narrations.</li>
            <li><strong>Abu Dawud, Tirmidhi, Nasa'i, & Darimi:</strong> Contain a mixture of <em>Sahih</em>, <em>Hasan</em> (Good), and some <em>Da'if</em> (Weak).</li>
            <li><strong>Ibn Majah & Musnad Ahmad:</strong> Include a slightly higher number of weak narrations.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            However, a weak (<em>Da'if</em>) Hadith in these collections does not mean it is fabricated; it simply means a narrator had a slightly weaker memory. Scholars still use these narrations for historical context and general advice, though not for strict legal rulings.
        </p>

        <div class="hadith-box">
            <p class="mb-0" style="font-style: italic;">The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: "Hold fast to my Sunnah and the Sunnah of the rightly-guided Caliphs after me. Bite onto it with your molar teeth."</p>
            <small class="text-muted d-block mt-2">— Sunan Abi Dawud</small>
        </div>

        <h3>How to Study the Major Collections</h3>
        <p>
            For beginners, the most practical approach is to start with <em>Sahih al-Bukhari</em> and <em>Sahih Muslim</em> through their English translations. For deeper study, the <em>40 Hadith of Imam Nawawi</em> (which draws from the Nine Books) is the perfect gateway to understanding their structure and content. As you progress, acquiring a physical copy of <em>Riyad as-Salihin</em> (also by Imam Nawawi) will allow you to explore these collections through the lens of ethics, spirituality, and daily practice.
        </p>

        <div class="quran-quote">
            "O you who have believed, obey Allah and obey the Messenger and those in authority among you..."
            <span class="quran-ref">Surah An-Nisa, 4:59</span>
        </div>

        <h3>Conclusion</h3>
        <p>
            The Nine Major Hadith Collections represent centuries of intellectual sacrifice, travel, and devotion to the Prophet ﷺ. They are not just books—they are the living bridge that connects modern Muslims to the guidance of the Messenger. By studying them, we do not just learn about history; we learn how to live.
        </p>
    </div>
